(CHAPTER I) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE EPITAPH OF SUMMER) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As I started out from the farm with a basket of potatoes, for our supper) Tj T*
(in the shack half a mile up the hillside, where we had made our Summer) Tj T*
(camp, my eye fell on a notice affixed to a gate-post, and, as I read it,) Tj T*
(my heart sank--sank as the sun was sinking yonder with wistful glory) Tj T*
(behind the purple ridge. I tore the paper from the gate-post and put it) Tj T*
(in my pocket with a sigh.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("It is true, then," I said to myself. "We have got to admit it. I must) Tj T*
(show this to Colin.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Then I continued my way across the empty, close-gleaned corn-field,) Tj T*
(across the railway track, and, plunging into the orchard on the other) Tj T*
(side, where here and there among the trees the torrents of apples were) Tj T*
(being already caught in boxes by the thrifty husbandman, began to breast) Tj T*
(the hill intersected with thickly wooded watercourses.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(High up somewhere amid the cloud of beeches and buttonwood trees, our log) Tj T*
(cabin lay hid, in a gully made by the little stream that filled our pails) Tj T*
(with a silver trickle over a staircase of shelving rock, and up there) Tj T*
(Colin was already busy with his skilled French cookery, preparing our) Tj T*
(evening meal. The woods still made a pompous show of leaves, but I knew) Tj T*
(it to be a hollow sham, a mask of foliage soon to be stripped off by) Tj T*
ET
