() Tj T*
(Yes! It was true. Summer was beginning to pack up, the great) Tj T*
(stage-carpenter was about to change the scene, and the great theatre was) Tj T*
(full of echoes and sighs and sounds of farewell. Of course, we had known) Tj T*
(it for some time, but had not had the heart to admit it to each other,) Tj T*
(could not find courage to say that one more golden Summer was at an end.) Tj T*
(But the paper I had torn from the roadside left us no further shred of) Tj T*
(illusion. There was an authoritative announcement there was no blinking,) Tj T*
(a notice to quit there was no gain-saying.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As I came to the crest of the hill, and in sight of the shack, shining) Tj T*
(with early lamp-light deep down among the trees of the gully, I could see) Tj T*
(Colin innocently at work on a salad, and hear him humming to himself his) Tj T*
(eternal "_Vive le Capitaine_.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was too pathetic. I believe the tears came to my eyes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("Colin," I said, as I at length arrived and set down my basket of) Tj T*
(potatoes, "read this.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(He took the paper from my hand and read:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("_Sun-up Baseball Club. September_ 19, 1908. _Last Match of the Season_") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(He knew what I meant.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("Yes!" he said. "It is the epitaph of Summer.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CHAPTER II) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(AT EVENING I CAME TO THE WOOD) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(My solitude had been kindly lent to me for the Summer by a friend, the) Tj T*
(prophet-proprietor of a certain famous Well of Truth some four miles) Tj T*
(away, whither souls flocked from all parts of America to drink of the) Tj T*
(living waters. I had been feeling town-worn and world-weary, and my) Tj T*
(friend had written me saying: "At Elim are twelve wells and seventy) Tj T*
(palm-trees," and so to Elim I had betaken myself. After a brief sojourn) Tj T*
(there, drinking of the waters, and building up on the strong diet of the) Tj T*
(sage's living words, he had given me the key to some green woods and) Tj T*
(streams of his, and bade me take them for my hermitage. I had a great) Tj T*
(making-up to arrange with Nature, and I half wondered how she would) Tj T*
(receive me after all this long time. But when did that mother ever turn) Tj T*
