() Tj T*
(_At evening I came to the wood, and threw myself on the breast) Tj T*
(  Of the great green mother, weeping, and the arms of a thousand trees) Tj T*
(Waved and rustled in welcome, and murmured: "Rest--rest--rest!) Tj T*
(  The leaves, thy brothers, shall heal thee; thy sisters, the flowers,) Tj T*
(bring peace.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At length I stayed from my weeping, and lifted my face from the grass;) Tj T*
(  The moon was walking the wood with feet of mysterious pearl,) Tj T*
(And the great trees held their breath, trance-like, watching her pass,) Tj T*
(  And a bird called out from the shadows, with voice as sweet as a girl.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(And then, in the holy silence, to the great green mother I prayed:) Tj T*
(  "Take me again to thy bosom, thy son who so close to thee,) Tj T*
(Aforetime, filial clung, then into the city strayed--) Tj T*
(  The painted face of the town, the wine and the harlotry.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("Bathe me in lustral dawns, and the morning star and the dew.) Tj T*
(  Make pure my heart as a bird and innocent as a flower,) Tj T*
(Make sweet my thoughts as the meadow-mint) Tj T*
(  --O make me all anew,) Tj T*
(And in the strength of beech and oak gird up my will with power.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("I have wandered far, O my mother, but here I return at the last,) Tj T*
(  Never again to stray in pilgrimage wanton and wild;) Tj T*
(A broken heart and a contrite here at thy feet I cast,) Tj T*
(  O take me back to thy bosom ..." And the mother answered, "Child!"_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was a wonderful reconciliation, a wonderful home-coming, and how I) Tj T*
(luxuriated in the great green forgiveness! Yes! the giant maples had) Tj T*
(forgiven me, and the multitudinous beeches had taken me to their arms.) Tj T*
(The flowers and I were friends again, the grass was my brother, and the) Tj T*
(shy nymph-like stream, dropping silver vowels into the silence, was my) Tj T*
(sweetheart.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
