(For those who value it, there is no form of property that inspires a) Tj T*
(sense of ownership so jealous as solitude. Rob my orchard if you will,) Tj T*
(but beware how you despoil me of my silence. The average noisy person can) Tj T*
(have no conception what a brutal form of trespass his coarsely cheerful) Tj T*
(voice may be in the exquisite spiritual hush of the woods, or what) Tj T*
(shattering discomfort his irrelevant presence in the landscape.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One day, to my horror, a picnic ruthlessly invaded my sanctuary. With a) Tj T*
(roar of Boeotian hilarity, it tore up the hillside as if it were a) Tj T*
(storming party, and half a day the sacred woods were vocal with silly) Tj T*
(catcalls and snatches of profane song. I locked up my hermitage, and,) Tj T*
(taking my stick, sought refuge in flight, like the other woodland) Tj T*
(creatures; only coming back at evening with cautious step and peering) Tj T*
(glance, half afraid lest it should still be there. No! It was gone, but) Tj T*
(its voices seemed to have left gaping wounds across the violated air, and) Tj T*
(the trees to wear a look of desecration. But presently the moon arose and) Tj T*
(washed the solitude clean again, and the wounds of silence were healed in) Tj T*
(the still night.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Next morning I amused myself by writing the following notice, which) Tj T*
(I nailed up on a great elm-tree standing guard at the beginning of) Tj T*
(the woods:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(               SILENCE!) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Speaking above a whisper in these woods) Tj T*
(         is forbidden by law_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This notice seems to have had its effect, for from this time on no more) Tj T*
(hands of marauders invaded my peace. But I had one other case of) Tj T*
(trespass, of which it is now time to speak.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Some short distance from the shack was a clearing in the woods, a) Tj T*
(thriving wilderness of bramble-bushes, poke-berries, myrtle-berries,) Tj T*
(mandrakes, milkweed, mullein, daisies and what not--a paradise of every) Tj T*
(sauntering vine and splendid, saucy weed. In the centre stood a) Tj T*
(sycamore-tree, beneath which it was my custom to smoke a morning pipe and) Tj T*
(revolve my profound after-breakfast thoughts.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Judge, then, of my indignant shock, one morning, at finding a stranger) Tj T*
(calmly occupying my place. I stood for a moment rooted to the spot, in) Tj T*
(the shadow of the encircling woods, and he had not yet seen me. As I) Tj T*
(stood, pondering on the best way of dealing with the intruder, a sudden) Tj T*
(revulsion of kindness stole over me. For here indeed was a very different) Tj T*
(figure from what, in my first shock of surprise, I had expected to see.) Tj T*
(No common intruder this. In fact, who could have dreamed of coming upon) Tj T*
(so incongruous an apparition as this in an American woodland? How on) Tj T*
(earth did this picturesque waif from the Quartier Latin come to stray so) Tj T*
(far away from the Boul' Miche! For the little boyish figure of a man that) Tj T*
(sat sketching in my place was the Frenchiest-looking Frenchman you ever) Tj T*
(saw--with his dark, smoke-dried skin, his long, straight, blue-black) Tj T*
(hair, his fine, rather ferocious brown eyes, his long, delicate French) Tj T*
