("Do you remember that first salad you made us, Colin?" I said, as we sat) Tj T*
(over our coffee, and Colin was filling his little pipe. "A daring work of) Tj T*
(art, a fantastic _tour de force_, of apples, and lettuce, and wild) Tj T*
(strawberries, and I don't know what else.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
("I believe I mixed in some May-apples, too. It was a great stunt ...) Tj T*
(well, no more May-apples and strawberries this year," he finished, with a) Tj T*
(sigh, and we both sat silently smoking, thinking over the good Summer) Tj T*
(that was gone.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(After our first meeting, Colin had dropped in to see me again from time) Tj T*
(to time, and when his work at the great house was finished, I had asked) Tj T*
(him to come and share my solitude. A veritable child of Nature himself,) Tj T*
(he fitted into my quiet days as silently as a squirrel. So much of his) Tj T*
(life had been passed out-of-doors with trees and skies, long dream-like) Tj T*
(days all alone sketching in solitary places, that he seemed as much a) Tj T*
(part of the woods as though he were a faun, and the lore of the elements,) Tj T*
(and all natural things--bugs and birds, all wildwood creatures--had) Tj T*
(passed into him with unconscious absorption. A sort of boyish) Tj T*
(unconsciousness, indeed, was the keynote and charm of his nature. A less) Tj T*
(sophisticated creature never followed the mystic calling of art.) Tj T*
(Fortunately for me, he was not one of those painters who understand and) Tj T*
(expound their own work. On the contrary, he was a perfect child about it,) Tj T*
(and painted for no more mysterious reason than that his eye delighted in) Tj T*
(beautiful natural effects, and that he loved to play with paint and) Tj T*
(brushes. Though he was undoubtedly sensitive somewhere to the mystic side) Tj T*
(of Nature, her Wordsworthian "intimations," you would hardly have guessed) Tj T*
(it from his talk. "A bully bit of colour," would be his craftsmanlike way) Tj T*
(of describing a twilight full of sibylline suggestiveness to the literary) Tj T*
(mind. But, strangely enough, when he brought you his sketch, all your) Tj T*
("sibylline suggestiveness" was there, which of course means, after all,) Tj T*
(that painting was his way of seeing and saying it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The moon rose as we smoked on, and began to lattice with silver the) Tj T*
(darkness of the glen, and flood the hillside with misty radiance. Colin) Tj T*
(made for his sketch-box.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
