(Orchards! We were walking to New York--through orchards. And we might) Tj T*
(have gone by train! A country of orchards and gold-dust sunshine falling) Tj T*
(through the quaint tapestry trees, falling dreamily on heaped-up gold,) Tj T*
(and the grave backs of little pigs joyously at large in the apple) Tj T*
(twilight. A drowsy, murmuring spell was on the land, the spell of fabled) Tj T*
(orchards, and of old enchanted gardens--) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_In the afternoon they came unto a land) Tj T*
(In which it seemed always afternoon_--) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(the country of King Alcinous. At intervals, as we walked on through the) Tj T*
(cider-dreamy afternoon, thinking apples, smelling apples, munching) Tj T*
(apples, there came a mellow sound like soft thunder through the trees. It) Tj T*
(was the thunder of apples being poured into barrels, and, as in a sleep,) Tj T*
(the fragrant wagons passed and repassed along the road--"the slow-moving) Tj T*
(wagons of our lady of Eleusis.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
