(plows and harrows, of mattocks and hurdles, and the mystical winnowing) Tj T*
(fan of Iacchus.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To the meditative, romantic mind, the farmer and plowman, standing thus) Tj T*
(in the foreground of the infinite perspective of time, take on a sacred) Tj T*
(significance, as of traditional ministers of the ancient mysteries of) Tj T*
(the earth.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Perhaps it is one's involuntary sense of this haunted antiquity that) Tj T*
(gives its peculiar expressiveness to the solemn, almost religious quiet) Tj T*
(of barns and stables, the, so to say, prehistoric hush of brooding,) Tj T*
(sun-steeped rickyards; and gives, too, a homely, sacerdotal look to the) Tj T*
(implements and vessels of the farm. A churn or a cheese-press gives one) Tj T*
(the same deep, uncanny thrill of the terrible vista of time as Stonehenge) Tj T*
(itself; and from such implements, too, there seems to breathe a sigh--a) Tj T*
(sigh of the long travail and unbearable pathos of the race of men.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(You will thus see the satisfaction, in moods of such meditation, of) Tj T*
(carrying in one's knapsack a line from Virgil--"the slow-moving wagons of) Tj T*
(our Lady of Eleusis"--and I congratulated myself on my forethought in) Tj T*
(having included in our itinerant library a copy of Mr. Mackail's) Tj T*
(beautiful translation of "The Georgics." Walt Whitman, talking to one of) Tj T*
(his friends about his habit of carrying a book with him on his nature) Tj T*
