(Perhaps some reader had been disposed hastily to say: "What did you want) Tj T*
(with hooks out of doors? Was not Nature enough?" No one who loves both) Tj T*
(books and Nature would ask that question, or need to have explained why a) Tj T*
(knapsack library is a necessary adjunct of a walking-tour.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(For Nature and books react so intimately on each other, and, far more) Tj T*
(than one realizes without thought, our enjoyment of Nature is a creation) Tj T*
(of literature. For example, can any one sensitive to such considerations) Tj T*
(deny that the meadows of the world are greener for the Twenty-third) Tj T*
(Psalm, or the starry sky the gainer in our imagination by the solemn) Tj T*
(cadences of the book of Job? All our experiences, new and personal as) Tj T*
(they may seem to us, owe incalculably their depth and thrill to the) Tj T*
(ancestral sentiment in our blood, and joy and sorrow are for us what they) Tj T*
(are, no little because so many old, far-away generations of men and women) Tj T*
(have joyed and sorrowed in the same way before us. Literature but) Tj T*
(represents that concentrated sentiment, and satisfies through expression) Tj T*
(our human need for some sympathetic participation with us in our human) Tj T*
(experience.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(That a long-dead poet walking in the Spring was moved as I am by the) Tj T*
(unfolding leaf and the returning bird imparts an added significance to my) Tj T*
(own feelings; and that some wise and beautiful old book knew and said it) Tj T*
(all long ago, makes my life seem all the more mysteriously romantic for) Tj T*
(me to-day. Besides, books are not only such good companions for what they) Tj T*
(say, but for what they are. As with any other friend, you may go a whole) Tj T*
(day with them, and not have a word to say to each other, yet be happily) Tj T*
(conscious of a perfect companionship. The book we know and love--and, of) Tj T*
(course, one would never risk taking a book we didn't know for a) Tj T*
(companion--has long since become a symbol for us, a symbol of certain) Tj T*
(moods and ways of feeling, a key to certain kingdoms of the spirit, of) Tj T*
(which it is often sufficient just to hold the key in our hands. So, a) Tj T*
(single flower in the hand is a key to Summer, a floating perfume the key) Tj T*
(to the hidden gardens of remembrance. The wrong book in the hand, whether) Tj T*
(opened or not, is as distracting a presence as an irrelevant person; and) Tj T*
(therefore it was with great care that I chose my knapsack library. It) Tj T*
(consisted of these nine books:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Mackail's "Georgics.") Tj T*
(Hans Andersen's Fairy Tales.) Tj T*
(Shakespeare's Sonnets.) Tj T*
(Locke's "Beloved Vagabond.") Tj T*
(Selections from R.L.S.) Tj T*
(Pater's "Marius the Epicurean.") Tj T*
(Alfred de Musset's "Premières Poésies.") Tj T*
(Baedeker's "United States.") Tj T*
(Road Map of New York State.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(And, though my knapsack already weighed eighteen pounds, I could not) Tj T*
(resist the call of a cheap edition of Wordsworth in a drug-store at) Tj T*
(Warsaw, a charming little town embosomed among hills and orchards, where) Tj T*
(we arrived, dreamy with country air, at the end of the day.) Tj T*
