() Tj T*
(FELLOW WAYFARERS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(With the morn our way still lay among apples and honey, hives and) Tj T*
(orchards; a land of prosperous farms, sumptuous rolling downs, rich) Tj T*
(woodland, sheep, more pigs, more apple-barrels and velvety sunshine. The) Tj T*
(old ruined houses had ceased, and the country had taken on a more) Tj T*
(generous, broad-shouldered, deep-bosomed aspect. Nature was preparing for) Tj T*
(one of her big Promised Land effects. We were coming to the valley of the) Tj T*
(Genesee River. We made a comparison of two kinds of prosperity in the) Tj T*
(look of a landscape. Some villages and farms suggest smugness in their) Tj T*
(prosperity. They have a model-farm, business-like, well-regulated,) Tj T*
(up-to-date, company-financed air, suggesting such modern agricultural) Tj T*
(terms as "ensilage," "irrigation" and "fertilizer." Other villages and) Tj T*
(farms, while just as well-kept and well-to-do, have, so to say, a) Tj T*
(something romantic about their prosperity, a bounteous, ruddy, golden-age) Tj T*
(look about them, as though Nature herself had been the farmer and they) Tj T*
(had ruddied and ripened out of her own unconscious abundance--the) Tj T*
(difference between a row of modern box beehives and the old) Tj T*
(thatched-cottage kind. The countryside of the Genesee valley has the) Tj T*
(romantic prosperous look. Its farms and villages look like farms and) Tj T*
(villages in picture-books, and the country folk we met seemed happy and) Tj T*
(gay and kind, such as those one reads of in William Morris's romances of) Tj T*
(the golden age. As from time to time we exchanged greetings with them, we) Tj T*
(were struck with their comely health and blithe ways--particularly with) Tj T*
(their fine teeth, as they laughed us the time of day, or stopped their) Tj T*
(wagons to gossip a moment with the two outlandish packmen--the very teeth) Tj T*
(one would expect in an apple-country. Perhaps they came of so much sweet) Tj T*
(commerce with apples!) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The possessor of a particularly fine display hailed us as he drove by in) Tj T*
(an empty wagon, at the tail of which trailed a long orchard ladder, and) Tj T*
(asked us if we would care for a lift. Now it happened that his) Tj T*
(suggestion came like a voice from heaven for poor Colin, one of whose) Tj T*
(shoes had been casting a gloom over our spirits for several miles. So we) Tj T*
(accepted with alacrity, and, really, riding felt quite good for a) Tj T*
(change! Our benefactor was a bronzed, handsome young fellow, just) Tj T*
(through Cornell, he told us, and proud of his brave college, as all) Tj T*
(Cornell men are. He had chosen apple-farming for his career, and,) Tj T*
(naturally, seemed quite happy about it; lived on his farm near by with) Tj T*
(his mother and sister, and was at the moment out on the quest of four) Tj T*
(apple-packers for his harvesting, these experts being at a premium at) Tj T*
(this season. We rattled along gaily in the broad afternoon sunshine,) Tj T*
(exchanging various human information, from apple-packing to New York) Tj T*
(theatres, after the manner of the companionable soul of man, and I hope) Tj T*
(he liked us as well as we liked him.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
