stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(banks lifted up our hearts. She, like ourselves, was on her way to) Tj T*
(join the Susquehanna, a mile or two below, and we said to ourselves,) Tj T*
(that, beautiful as the land had been through which we had already) Tj T*
(passed, we were now entering on a Nature of more heroic mould,) Tj T*
(mightier contours, and larger aspects. We were henceforth to walk in) Tj T*
(the company of great rivers: the Susquehanna, like some epic goddess,) Tj T*
(was to lead us to the Lehigh; the Blue Mountains were to bring us to) Tj T*
(the Delaware; and the uplands of Sullivan County were to bring us) Tj T*
(to--the lordly gates of the Hudson.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Our chests expanded as imagination luxuriated in the pictures it made.) Tj T*
(Our walk was only just beginning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CHAPTER XXIV) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(AND UNEXPECTEDLY THE LAST) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We had seen the two great rivers sweep into each other's arms in a broad) Tj T*
(glory of sunlit water, meeting at the bosky end of a wooded promontory,) Tj T*
(and yes! there was the Susquehanna glittering far beneath--the beautiful) Tj T*
(name I had so often seen and wondered about, painted on the sides of) Tj T*
(giant freight-cars! Yes, there was actually the great legendary river. It) Tj T*
(was a very warm, almost sultry noonday, more like midsummer than) Tj T*
(mid-October, and the river was almost blinding in its flashing beauty.) Tj T*
(Loosening our knapsacks, we called a halt and, leaning over the railing) Tj T*
(guarding the precipitous bank, luxuriated in the visionary scene. So) Tj T*
(high was the bank, and so broad the river, that we seemed lifted up into) Tj T*
(space, and the river, dreamily flowing beneath a gauze veil of heat-mist,) Tj T*
(seemed miles below us and drowsily unreal. Its course inshore was dotted) Tj T*
(with boulders, in the shadows of which we could see long ghostly fishes) Tj T*
(lazily gliding, and a mud-turtle, with a trail of little ones, slowly) Tj T*
(moving from rock to rock.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Suddenly Colin put his hand to his head, and swayed toward me, as though) Tj T*
(he were about to faint.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("I don't know what's the matter, old man," he said, "but I think I had) Tj T*
(better sit down a minute." And he sank by the roadside.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Unlike himself, he had been complaining of fatigue, and had seemed out of) Tj T*
(sorts for a day or two, but we had thought nothing of it; and, after) Tj T*
(resting a few minutes, he announced himself ready for the road again,) Tj T*
(but he looked very pale and walked with evident weariness. As a roadside) Tj T*
(cottage came in sight, "I wonder if they could give us a cup of tea," he) Tj T*
(said; "that would fix me up, I'm sure." So we knocked, and the door was) Tj T*
(opened by a pathetic shadow of an old woman, very poor and thin and) Tj T*
(weary-looking, who, although, as we presently learned, she was at the) Tj T*
(moment suffering from the recent loss of one eye, made us welcome and) Tj T*
ET
