110 0 obj
<< /Length 2932 >>
stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(busied herself about tea, with an unselfish kindness that touched our) Tj T*
(hearts, and made us reflect on the angelic goodness of human) Tj T*
(nature--sometimes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(She looked anxiously, mother-like, at Colin, and persuaded him to lie) Tj T*
(down and rest awhile in her little parlour, and, while he rested, she and) Tj T*
(I talked and she told me how she had come by her blind eye--an odd,) Tj T*
(harmless-sounding cause. She had been looking up into one of her) Tj T*
(apple-trees, one day, a few weeks ago, and an apple had fallen and struck) Tj T*
(her in the eye. Such innocent means does Nature sometimes use for her) Tj T*
(cruel accidents of disease and death! Just an apple falling from a) Tj T*
(tree,--and you are blind! A fly stings you, on a Summer day, and you die.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Colin, rested and refreshed, we once more started on our way, but,) Tj T*
(bravely as he strode on, there was no disguising it--my blithe,) Tj T*
(happy-hearted companion was ill. Of course we both assured the other that) Tj T*
(it could be nothing, but privately our hearts sank with a vague fear we) Tj T*
(did not speak. At length, after a weary four miles, we reached Towanda.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("I'm afraid," said poor Colin, "I can walk no more to-day. Perhaps a good) Tj T*
(night's rest will make me all right." We found an inn, and while Colin) Tj T*
(threw himself, wearied, on his bed, I went out, not telling him, and) Tj T*
(sought a doctor.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("And you've been walking with this temperature?" said the learned man,) Tj T*
(when he had seated himself at Colin's bedside and felt his wrist. "Have) Tj T*
(you been drinking much water as you went along? ... H'm--it's been a very) Tj T*
(dry Summer, you know.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(And the words of our friend in the buggy came back to us with sickening) Tj T*
(emphasis. O those innocent-looking fairy wells and magic mirrors by the) Tj T*
(road-side! And I thought, too, of the poor old blinded woman and the) Tj T*
(falling apple. Was Nature really like that?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(And then the wise man's verdict fell on our ears like a doom.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("Take my advice, and don't walk any more, but catch the night train for) Tj T*
(New York.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Poor Colin! But there was no appeal.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The end of our trip had come, suddenly, unreasonably, stupidly,) Tj T*
(like this.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("So we've got to be shot into New York like a package through a tube,) Tj T*
(after all!" said Colin. "No lordly gates of the Hudson for us! What a) Tj T*
(fool I feel, to be the one to spoil our trip like this!") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(And the tears glistened in our eyes, as we pressed each other's hand in) Tj T*
(that dreary inn bedroom, with the shadow of we knew not what for Colin) Tj T*
(over us--for our comradeship had been very good, day by day, together on) Tj T*
(the open road.) Tj T*
ET
