() Tj T*
(Our train did not go till midnight, so we had a long melancholy evening) Tj T*
(before us; but the doctor had given Colin some mysterious potion) Tj T*
(containing rest, and presently, as I sat by his side in the gray) Tj T*
(twilight, he fell into a deep sleep--a sleep, alas! of fire and wandering) Tj T*
(talk. It was pitiful to hear him, poor fellow--living over again in) Tj T*
(dreams the road we had travelled, or making pictures of the road he) Tj T*
(still dreamed ahead of us. Never before had I realized how entirely his) Tj T*
(soul was the soul of a painter--all pictures and colour.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("O my God!" he would suddenly exclaim, "did you ever see such blue in) Tj T*
(your life!" and then again, evidently referring to some particularly) Tj T*
(attractive effect in the phantasmagoria of his fever, "it's no use--you) Tj T*
(must let me stop and have a shot to get that, before it goes.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One place that seemed particularly to haunt him was--Mauch Chunk. He had) Tj T*
(been there before, and, as we had walked along, had often talked) Tj T*
(enthusiastically of it. "Wait till we get to Mauch Chunk," he said; "then) Tj T*
(the real fun will begin." And now, over and over again, he kept making) Tj T*
(pictures of Mauch Chunk, till I could have cried.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("Dramatic black rocks," he would murmur, "water rushing from the hills) Tj T*
(in every direction--clean-cut, vivid scenery--like theatres--the road) Tj T*
(runs by the side of a steel-blue river at the bottom of a chasm, and) Tj T*
(there is hardly room for it--the houses cling to the hillside like) Tj T*
(swallows' nests--here and there patches of fresh green grass gleam among) Tj T*
(the rocks, and, high up in the air on some dizzy ledge, there is a wild) Tj T*
(apple-tree in blossom--it is all black rocks and springs and moss and) Tj T*
(tumbling water--") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Then again his soul was evidently walking in the Blue Mountains, and) Tj T*
(several times he repeated a phrase of mine that had taken his fancy: "And) Tj T*
(now for the spacious corridors of the Highlands, and the lordly gates of) Tj T*
(the Hudson.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Then he would suddenly half awaken and turn to me, realizing the) Tj T*
(truth, and say:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("O our beautiful journey--to end like this!" and fall asleep again.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(And once more I fell to thinking of fairy springs by the roadside, and) Tj T*
(apples falling innocently from the bough, and how the beautiful journey) Tj T*
(we call life might some day suddenly end like this, with half the) Tj T*
(beautiful road untravelled--the rest sleep and perchance dreams.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(       *       *       *       *       *) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But Colin did not die. He is once more painting out in the sun, and next) Tj T*
(year we plan to stand again on that very spot by the Susquehanna, and) Tj T*
(watch the shadows of great fishes gliding through the dreamy water, and) Tj T*
(the mud-turtle with her trail of little ones moving from rock to) Tj T*
(rock--and then we shall strike out on the road again, just where we left) Tj T*
